{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And afterward you may come to her and possess her.</b> They said in the Sifre (Ki Teitzei 13): you have nothing with her except the act of intercourse. They intended to say that kiddushei kesef (betrothal by money) and a shetar (deed) do not take effect with her, and also the act of intercourse is not for the sake of kiddushin, for her legal status is still like that of a gentile woman upon whom kiddushin do not take effect, but rather Scripture permits him through the act of intercourse with her. And since it says 'and she shall be to you for a wife,' this indicates that she is his wife in the full sense, and if she commits adultery under him, she is judged as a married woman. And thus they said in the Gemara in tractate Qiddushin (Qiddushin 22a): 'and you shall take for yourself for a wife' — you have acquisition of her, that is, after all these actions. And in the Sifre (there): 'and she shall be to you for a wife' — in the same matter as is stated: 'her food, her clothing, and her conjugal rights he shall not diminish.' If so, she is his wife completely, for the Torah acquired her for him.\n\nAnd according to the plain sense, it would appear that it is forbidden to have intercourse with her until he performs all of this entire Torah, and this is the meaning of 'and afterward you may come to her and possess her.' And what it says, 'and you desire her and take her for yourself for a wife' — that he should take her to be for him as a wife after these actions which He commands, as in 'and I took her for myself for a wife' (Genesis 12:19).\n\nBut in the Gemara in tractate Qiddushin (Qiddushin 21b) they said that he is permitted to come upon her in a first act of intercourse, and this is 'and you shall take for yourself for a wife.' And they interpreted (there, 22a): 'and you shall bring her into the midst of your house' — this teaches that he must not press her in the war. They joined it above: 'and you shall take for yourself for a wife' when you bring her into the midst of your house, and behold he is permitted to take her in war and bring her to his house and to his city, and to have intercourse with her to break his lustful heart, and after the first act of intercourse she becomes forbidden to him until he performs for her all of this entire Torah. And this is the way of the Gemara in many places.\n\nBut I saw in the Yerushalmi in tractate Sanhedrin (see Yerushalmi Makkot 2:6): Rabbi Yohanan sent to the rabbis there: 'You say two matters in the name of Rav, and they are not so. You say in the name of Rav: a beautiful woman was permitted only in a first act of intercourse alone; but I say: not a first act of intercourse and not a second one, but only after all these actions — \"and afterward you may come to her and possess her,\" after all these actions.' And this is the plain meaning of the verse."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase 'תרין מילין אתון אמרין בשם רב ולית אינון כן' is rendered literally as 'two matters you say in the name of Rav and they are not so,' preserving Ramban's direct quotation of the Yerushalmi's Aramaic."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:52:50.961883960Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}